<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.4: Making a Perfect Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02346</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.4: Making a Perfect Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bfab2c94-0ccb-49c6-a68c-732fc88a9145</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>For questions 1 – 3, what could be added to each expression to make it a perfect square?</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2+12x\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 36
  The constant term \(c=36\) can be added to make the perfect square \(x^2+12x+36\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2−6x+1\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 8
  8 can be added to the constant term \(c=1\) to make the perfect square \(x^2-6x+9\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2+14x−10\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 59
  59 can be added to the constant term \(c=-10\) to make the perfect square \(x^2+14x+49\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \(x^2−16x=-60\) by completing the square. Show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>
  \(\begin{aligned} x^{2}-16 x &amp;=-60 \\ x^{2}-16 x+64 &amp;=-60+64 \\ x^{2}-16 x+64 &amp;=4 \\ (x-8)^{2} &amp;=4 \\ \sqrt{(x-8)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{4} \\ x-8 &amp;=\pm 2 \\ x-8 &amp;=2 \\ x-8 &amp;=-2 \\ \\ x=6 &amp; \text { or } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para></section>
</content>
